      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment carefully.\n\nThis is part 2 of 2 — a fragment of one certificate: OIML R76/2006-A-GB1-18.07.\n\nThe certificate number: R76/2006-A-GB1-18.07\n- Recommendation: R76 (canonical \"R76\"), edition 2006, scheme A\n- Page total: 5 (from \"Page 4 of 5 pages\" and \"Page 5 of 5 pages\")\n\nContent present in this fragment:\n1. Continuation of description with features (feet, level indicator, LCD displays, EX-link converter) — partial, part of description\n2. Devices list\n3. Interfaces\n4. Load cell info (Bizerba strain gauge load cell)\n5. Technical data (5 V DC power supply via USB)\n6. A technical data table with two types: PMA-EV and PMA-HD\n   - Models: \n     - PMA-EV: EVO1S1N3CI-C, EVO1S1CI-C, EVO1Y1N3CI-C, EVO1Y1CI-C, EVO1X1N3CI-C, EVO1X1CI-C, EVO1X2N3CI-C, EVO1X2CI-C, EVO1X3N3CI-C, EVO1X3CI-C, EVO1X4N3CI-C, EVO1X4CI-C, VIS1Y1M3CI-C, VIS1Y1CI-C, VIS1Y2M3CI-C, VIS1Y2CI-C, VIS1Y3M3CI-C, VIS1Y3CI-C, VIS1Y4M3CI-C, VIS1Y4CI-C, VIS1X1M3CI-C, VIS1X1CI-C, VIS1X2M3CI-C, VIS1X2CI-C, VIS1X3M3CI-C, VIS1X3CI-C, VIS1X4M3CI-C, VIS1X4CI-C\n     - PMA-HD: SPO1S1N3CI-C, SPO1S1CI-C, LAB1X1N3CI-C, LAB1X1CI-C, LAB1X2N3CI-C, LAB1X2CI-C, LAB1X3N3CI-C, LAB1X3CI-C, LAB1X4N3CI-C, LAB1X4CI-C\n   - Class: II (PMA-EV), III (PMA-HD)\n   - Max: 7500 g (PMA-EV), 2200 g (PMA-HD)\n   - Min: 5 g (PMA-EV), 20 g (PMA-HD)\n   - e = 1 g both\n   - d = 0.1 g (PMA-EV), 1 g (PMA-HD)\n   - T ≤ - Max (i.e., T ≤ Max — tare)\n   - Temperature: +10 to +30 °C (PMA-EV), +10 to +40 °C (PMA-HD)\n   - Load Cell: Bizerba WS8 (PMA-EV), Bizerba WS3 (PMA-HD)\n   - Emax: 8 kg (PMA-EV), 3 kg (PMA-HD)\n7. Software: BAC software, ID 05496 / 1578 (VIS models), version 00-56-02.xx; Software applications APC (only VIS models), ID 6587, version 01-72-03.xx\n8. Sealing info\n\nNow, the fragment covers a family of models with two \"types\" (PMA-EV and PMA-HD). Attributes vary by type/model.\n\nHow to structure:\n\n- certificate: number \"R76/2006-A-GB1-18.07\", scheme \"A\", page_total 5. Recommendation R76 edition 2006.\n- recommendation: id \"R76\", edition 2006, scheme \"A\". Accuracy classes: II and III — but those vary per type. The recommendation section has accuracy_classes[]. We could include [\"II\", \"III\"]. Hmm, but class is per model family. It's safe to include accuracy_classes: [\"II\", \"III\"].\n\n- certified_type: category — this is a weighing instrument (non-automatic weighing instrument, R76). Category could be \"Weighing instrument\". The description mentions paint mixing software — these are paint mixing scales. Type designations: PMA-EV, PMA-HD and the models. Hmm, \"type_designations[] — all type/model identifiers mentioned\". So include PMA-EV, PMA-HD, and all the model IDs? That would be a lot. The schema says type_designations[] includes all type/model identifiers. But model_family is for >1 model variant — definitely applies here.\n\nLet me structure model_family:\n- family_name: \"PMA\" maybe? The types are PMA-EV and PMA-HD. Actually the family name might be PMA. But it's not explicitly given as family. Hmm. The types PMA-EV and PMA-HD are two type designations. Under each type, models are listed.\n\nThe schema: certified_type.type_designations[] — all type/model identifiers mentioned. model_family.models[] — one entry per variant with model_id, label, attributes{}.\n\nApproach: create models for each model_id, with attributes including type, class, max, min, e, d, temperature, load cell, emax.\n\nActually, I could structure it with two models entries: PMA-EV and PMA-HD as model_id, with attributes listing models. But the \"model_id\" should probably be the individual model identifiers. However, 38 model IDs would be a lot. Let me think about what's most useful.\n\nOption A: model_family.models = two entries: {model_id: \"PMA-EV\", attributes: {models: [...], class: \"II\", maximum_capacity: 7500 g, ...}} and {model_id: \"PMA-HD\", ...}.\n\nOption B: model_family.models = 38 entries, one per model, each with attributes referencing type PMA-EV/PMA-HD.\n\nThe characteristics model_level could have attribute per model... but the varying attribute axis is really the type (PMA-EV vs PMA-HD). The table rows are: Type | PMA-EV | PMA-HD. So the matrix axis is \"type\".\n\nI think the cleanest approach given the schema:\n- matrix_tables: one table \"Technical data by type\" with columns type, models, class, max, min, e, d, temperature, load_cell, emax, and two rows.\n- model_family.models: entries per model (38) would be verbose. Alternatively two entries keyed on type with a \"models\" attribute listing model IDs.\n\nHmm, the schema says models[]: one entry per variant, with model_id, label, attributes{}. The variants here — the individual model identifiers. But each model shares type-level attributes. I could list each model with attributes {type: \"PMA-EV\"} and then model_level characteristics keyed on model... that's 38 entries per attribute. Too verbose.\n\nAlternative: use model_id \"PMA-EV\" and \"PMA-HD\" as the two variants, with attributes containing the list of models. The model_level characteristics then have 2 values each (per type). That's cleaner and captures the data faithfully.\n\nGiven \"model_id, label, attributes{}\" — I'll do:\n\n```json\n\"model_family\": {\n  \"family_name\": \"PMA\",\n  \"models\": [\n    {\n      \"model_id\": \"PMA-EV\",\n      \"label\": \"PMA-EV\",\n      \"attributes\": {\n        \"class\": \"II\",\n        \"models\": [\"EVO1S1N3CI-C\", ...]\n      }\n    },\n    ...\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nHmm, is family_name \"PMA\"? Not explicitly stated as family name. The types are \"PMA-EV\" and \"PMA-HD\". I could set family_name to null and use model_id \"PMA-EV\"/\"PMA-HD\". Or infer \"PMA\" as family. Rule 8: don't fabricate. The PMA prefix is evident but \"family_name\" is \"explicit family name if any, else null\". So null... but maybe \"PMA\" is fine as it's the common prefix? To be safe, I'll set family_name to null and mention PMA-EV/PMA-HD in type_designations.\n\nActually wait — since part 1 of this certificate is not shown, the type might be defined elsewhere. In this fragment, \"Type: PMA-EV / PMA-HD\". So the certified types are PMA-EV and PMA-HD.\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Weighing instrument\" (R76 is non-automatic weighing instruments). The category examples include \"Indicator\", \"Load cell\", etc. For R76 scales, category \"Weighing instrument\" seems right.\n- type_designations: [\"PMA-EV\", \"PMA-HD\"] plus all models? The rule says all type/model identifiers mentioned. I'll include the two types, and let model_family carry the individual models. Actually, to be thorough, maybe include all models in type_designations too. That's 38 identifiers. Hmm. It could be reasonable. But it might be redundant. I'll include the two type designations and the model list in model_family attributes. Actually the schema says \"type_designations[] — all type/model identifiers mentioned\". Let me include all model IDs to comply. That's a lot of JSON but fine.\n\nLet me enumerate models carefully:\n\nPMA-EV:\n1. EVO1S1N3CI-C\n2. EVO1S1CI-C\n3. EVO1Y1N3CI-C\n4. EVO1Y1CI-C\n5. EVO1X1N3CI-C\n6. EVO1X1CI-C\n7. EVO1X2N3CI-C\n8. EVO1X2CI-C\n9. EVO1X3N3CI-C\n10. EVO1X3CI-C\n11. EVO1X4N3CI-C\n12. EVO1X4CI-C\n13. VIS1Y1M3CI-C\n14. VIS1Y1CI-C\n15. VIS1Y2M3CI-C\n16. VIS1Y2CI-C\n17. VIS1Y3M3CI-C\n18. VIS1Y3CI-C\n19. VIS1Y4M3CI-C\n20. VIS1Y4CI-C\n21. VIS1X1M3CI-C\n22. VIS1X1CI-C\n23. VIS1X2M3CI-C\n24. VIS1X2CI-C\n25. VIS1X3M3CI-C\n26. VIS1X3CI-C\n27. VIS1X4M3CI-C\n28. VIS1X4CI-C\n\nPMA-HD:\n1. SPO1S1N3CI-C\n2. SPO1S1CI-C\n3. LAB1X1N3CI-C\n4. LAB1X1CI-C\n5. LAB1X2N3CI-C\n6. LAB1X2CI-C\n7. LAB1X3N3CI-C\n8. LAB1X3CI-C\n9. LAB1X4N3CI-C\n10. LAB1X4CI-C\n\nTotal 38 models.\n\nmodel_level characteristics (attribute × model matrix). If I use PMA-EV/PMA-HD as the \"model\" keys in model_level values, then:\n\n- accuracy_class: II / III (or \"class\")\n- maximum_capacity (Max): 7500 g / 2200 g\n- minimum_capacity (Min): 5 g / 20 g\n- verification_scale_interval_e: 1 g / 1 g\n- actual_scale_interval_d: 0.1 g / 1 g\n- temperature_range: {min:10, max:30} °C / {min:10, max:40} °C\n- load_cell: \"Bizerba WS8\" / \"Bizerba WS3\"\n- load_cell_emax: 8 kg / 3 kg\n- tare: \"≤ Max\" — T ≤ - Max. This is \"T ≤ -Max\"? Actually \"T ≤ - Max\" — probably means T ≤ -Max? No, typically for these scales T ≤ 0.9 Max or similar. The cell says \"T ≤ - Max\" spanning both columns. Hmm, likely \"T ≤ Max\" (subtractive tare up to Max) — the dash might be an OCR artifact. I'll record tare as {value: \"≤ Max\"}... hmm, rule says pure footnote markers not values; this has content \"≤ Max\". Could record characteristic \"tare\" value \"≤ Max\" — maybe as \"T ≤ Max\". I'll record as \"tare_limit\" value \"≤ Max\" at type_level since it applies to both. Actually let me just record value \"≤ Max\" with unit null. Or skip? It's meaningful: tare ≤ Max. I'll include it in matrix table and as type_level characteristic \"maximum_tare\": {\"value\": \"Max\"}? Hmm. Let me record \"tare\" with value \"≤ Max\". Actually cleaner: attribute \"maximum_tare\" value \"≤ Max\". Hmm, or record value \"Max\" with attribute \"maximum_tare_limit\". I'll do attribute \"tare_limit\", value \"≤ Max\".\n\nmodel_family.models: I'll go with two entries (PMA-EV, PMA-HD), each with attributes {class, max, min, e, d, temperature, load_cell, emax} and a \"models\" list? The attributes{} is free-form. I could put \"included_models\": [...] in attributes. That's helpful.\n\nAlternatively, list each of the 38 models individually with attributes {type: \"PMA-EV\", accuracy_class: \"II\", ...} — duplicating 8 attributes 38 times. Verbose but complete. Hmm.\n\nThe instruction in the prompt: \"model_family: include ONLY if cert covers >1 model variant; models[]: one entry per variant\". The variants here — each model ID is a variant. But grouping by type is more aligned with the table structure. In the R60 example, they created 6 models keyed by Emax values. Here the natural key is the model ID, with the type as a grouping.\n\nLet me think about what a downstream consumer wants: ability to look up per-model data. With 38 models sharing 2 attribute sets, listing per-model with type reference is fine, and model_level characteristics could then use \"model\" keys of individual IDs — but that's 38 values per attribute. Too much.\n\nCompromise: model_family.models = 38 entries each with attributes {type: \"PMA-EV\", accuracy_class: \"II\"} — lightweight per-model. And model_level characteristics use type-level keys (PMA-EV / PMA-HD) for the physical characteristics, with attributes like \"applies_to_type\". Hmm, but then \"model\" in values would be \"PMA-EV\" which matches model_family model_id if I use the type as model_id...\n\nActually, simplest consistent approach: treat PMA-EV and PMA-HD as the two \"models\" (they're labeled \"Type\" in the table, but they function as variants). Each has a list of individual model identifiers in attributes. model_level values reference model_id PMA-EV/PMA-HD. And matrix_tables preserve the raw table with model lists.\n\nI'll go with that. It's clean, faithful, and parseable.\n\nAlso the earlier description bits (features, devices, interfaces) — these are part of description in certified_type. I should include them since this fragment contains them. The fragment starts mid-list (\"– 3 feet, 2 adjustable – Level indicator ...\"). I'll compose a description that includes the listed features, devices, interfaces, load cell, and technical data notes.\n\ncertified_type.description: I can write a structured description capturing:\n- Features: 3 feet, 2 adjustable; level indicator; LCD segment display with fixed position touch keys (models EVO1*, LAB1*, SPO1*); LCD graphic display with touch function (models VIS1*); EX-link converter for voltage supply and data transfer (models EVO1X*, LAB1X*, VIS1X*, VIS1Y*)\n- Devices: initial zero setting device (≤ 20% of Max); zero tracking device (≤ 4% of Max); zero indicator (models LAB1*, SPO1*); combined zero-setting and tare-balancing device; preset tare device (only via a PC); approximate displaying device (models EVO1*, VIS1*); weight accumulation; weight calculating\n- Interfaces: USB (direct on SPO1S*, EVO1S*, EVO1Y*; via Ex-Link converter on EVO1X*, LAB1X*); Ethernet via Ex-link converter (VIS1X*, VIS1Y*)\n- Load cell: one strain gauge load cell manufactured by Bizerba\n- Power: 5 V DC via USB; for LAB1X*, EVO1X*, VIS1X*, VIS1Y* power via Ex-link converter and RJ-45\n\ncharacteristics.type_level could include:\n- initial_zero_setting_range: {min: null, max: 20, unit: \"%\"}? Value \"≤ 20% of Max\" → value {max: 20}, unit \"%\"? The reference is Max. Per rule 3 example, \"150% of Emax\" → value 150, unit \"%\", separate reference. So: initial_zero_setting_range: {value: {min: null, max: 20}, unit_symbol: \"%\"} plus maybe reference \"Max\". I could encode value as \"≤ 20% of Max\" — but rules say numbers not strings when numeric. I'll do value {max: 20}, unit \"%\" and add a separate characteristic? That might be over-engineering. Alternatively record as string \"≤ 20% of Max\". The rules prefer structured. I'll do:\n  - \"initial_zero_setting_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 20}, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"}\n  - \"zero_tracking_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 4}, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"}\n  \n  Hmm but the \"of Max\" reference is lost. I could name them \"initial_zero_setting_range_percent_of_max\" — canonical labels should be clean. Maybe \"initial_zero_setting_range_of_max\" and \"zero_tracking_range_of_max\". That encodes the reference in the attribute name. Good.\n\n- power_supply_voltage: 5 V DC → \"power_supply_voltage\": {\"value\": 5, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"} and \"power_supply_type\": {\"value\": \"DC\"}. Also note provided by USB. Maybe \"power_supply\": {\"value\": \"5 V DC via USB\"}. I'll do voltage + type, and mention USB in description.\n\nmodel_level[] entries (keys