alues: {\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5}). I'll include unit_symbol on each value object to satisfy rule 2 while also keeping attribute-level unit — hmm, that's redundant. The R137 example is explicit and official: values are {\"model\": ..., \"value\": ...} with unit at attribute level. I'll follow that exact pattern but add footnote_markers only when present. Actually to be safe, I'll follow the rule-4 example format exactly.\n\nmatrix_tables: preserve the technical data table:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"Technical data by type\",\n  \"columns\": [\"type\", \"models\", \"accuracy_class\", \"maximum_capacity\", \"minimum_capacity\", \"verification_scale_interval\", \"actual_scale_interval\", \"tare_limit\", \"temperature_range\", \"load_cell\", \"load_cell_maximum_capacity\"],\n  \"column_units\": [null, null, null, \"g\", \"g\", \"g\", \"g\", null, \"°C\", null, \"kg\"],\n  \"rows\": [\n    {\"type\": \"PMA-EV\", \"models\": [...28...], \"accuracy_class\": \"II\", \"maximum_capacity\": 7500, \"minimum_capacity\": 5, \"verification_scale_interval\": 1, \"actual_scale_interval\": 0.1, \"tare_limit\": \"≤ Max\", \"temperature_range\": {\"min\": 10, \"max\": 30}, \"load_cell\": \"Bizerba WS8\", \"load_cell_maximum_capacity\": 8},\n    {\"type\": \"PMA-HD\", \"models\": [...10...], ...}\n  ],\n  \"description\": \"...\",\n  \"footnotes\": []\n}\n```\n\nSoftware: The software table — how to represent? Could be a component or characteristics. The weighing instrument software BAC: ID 05496 (1578 for VIS models), version 00-56-02.xx. Software application APC (only VIS models): ID 6587, version 01-72-03.xx.\n\nI could add matrix_tables for software, or type_level characteristics:\n- \"weighing_instrument_software_id\": {\"value\": \"05496\"} with note \"1578 for VIS models\" — hmm. Software ID varies: 05496 for non-VIS, 1578 for VIS. Could be model_level:\n  {\"attribute\": \"software_id\", \"values\": [{\"model\": \"VIS models\", \"value\": \"1578\"}, {\"model\": \"non-VIS models\", \"value\": \"05496\"}]}\n  \nOr simpler: type_level entries:\n- \"software_id_bac\": {\"value\": \"05496 (1578 for VIS models)\"} — string with parens, not great.\n\nLet me do model_level:\n```json\n{\"attribute\": \"instrument_software_id\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"values\": [{\"model\": \"PMA-EV (VIS models)\", \"value\": \"1578\"}, {\"model\": \"PMA-EV (non-VIS models)\", \"value\": \"05496\"}, {\"model\": \"PMA-HD\", \"value\": \"05496\"}]}\n```\nHmm, that's getting complicated. Software version 00-56-02.xx where xx = legally non-relevant changes — same for all.\n\nAlternatively include software as a second matrix_table \"Permitted software versions\":\ncolumns: software, software_id, software_version, applicable_models\nrows:\n- {software: \"Weighing instrument software BAC\", software_id: \"05496\", ...} — but VIS models have 1578.\n\nRows:\n1. {\"software\": \"Weighing instrument software BAC\", \"software_id\": \"05496\", \"software_id_note\": \"1578 for VIS models\", \"software_version\": \"00-56-02.xx\", \"applicable_models\": \"all models (VIS models: software ID 1578)\"}\n2. {\"software\": \"Software application APC\", \"software_id\": \"6587\", \"software_version\": \"01-72-03.xx\", \"applicable_models\": \"VIS models only\"}\n\nThat's a faithful preservation. I'll do that as a matrix_table named \"Permitted software versions\".\n\nOr components[]: software could be a component with role \"software\". Components are \"sub-assemblies\" — software versions could fit. I'll use matrix_tables for software; simpler.\n\nAlso, could add type_level characteristics for software:\n- \"instrument_software_version\": {\"value\": \"00-56-02.xx\"}\n- \"application_software_apc_version\": {\"value\": \"01-72-03.xx\", footnote/applicable VIS}\nHmm. The matrix table covers it. Maybe also add software ID type_level:\n- \"instrument_software_id\": {\"value\": \"05496\"} — but VIS models use 1578. I'll leave IDs in the matrix table only to avoid misrepresenting.\n\nSealing: \"Access to the electronics and load cell is prevented by two tamper evident labels on the underside of the instrument; and access to the Menu access switch is prevented by a tamper evident label covering the switch.\" → include in description or a type_level characteristic \"sealing\": {\"value\": \"Two tamper evident labels on the underside of the instrument; tamper evident label covering the Menu access switch\"}. I'll fold into description.\n\ncertificate fields:\n- number: \"R76/2006-A-GB1-18.07\"\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- page_total: 5\n- date_issued: not in fragment (part 1 presumably). Omit.\n- member_state: \"GB\" — the number contains \"-GB1-\". Hmm, is that member state? \"GB1\" — GB is United Kingdom. The cert number R76/2006-A-GB1-18.07 — GB likely indicates issuing country (UK). But I shouldn't over-infer... Actually it's reasonable: many OIML cert numbers embed the country code. But rule 8: don't fabricate. The fragment doesn't explicitly state member state. I'll omit to be safe. Hmm, part 1 would have it. Since this is \"a fragment of one certificate\", fields like applicants/manufacturers may be in part 1. The instruction: \"produce JSON for the content present in THIS fragment only. Omit sections not present.\" So omit member_state, applicants, manufacturers, issuing_authority, test_reports.\n\nWait — is Bizerba a manufacturer mentioned? \"manufactured by Bizerba\" (load cell) and load cells \"Bizerba WS8\"/\"WS3\". The manufacturer of the instrument is presumably Bizerba but not stated in this fragment. The load cell manufacturer is Bizerba. I won't add manufacturers[] since it's the load cell manufacturer, not necessarily stated as instrument manufacturer... Actually \"The instrument is fitted with one strain gauge load cell as per technical data, manufactured by Bizerba.\" — that says the load cell is manufactured by Bizerba. Components[] could record the load cell:\n\ncomponents:\n- role: \"load_cell\", type_designations: [\"Bizerba